==English==
{{was wotd|2013|February|21}}

===Etymology===
From "Hollywood (Part 3)", a 1977 episode during the fifth season of the American television series ''[[w:Happy Days|Happy Days]]'', in which the character Fonzie jumped over a shark on waterskis. The expression argues that it was at this point that the show bore no similarity to its original form.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-jump the shark.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic|of a [[television program]] or other [[narrative]]}} To undergo a [[storyline]] development which heralds a fundamental and generally [[disappointing]] change in [[direction]].
<!--#: ''Happy Days '''jumped the shark''' when Fonzie jumped over a shark on waterskis.'' - it's not possible to use the origin of an idiom as an example of the idiom-->
#* '''2003''', Jon Hein, ''Jump the Shark'', <span class="plainlinks">[http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=TPyzvSlPEwsC&q=%22jump+the+shark%22&dq=%22jump+the+shark%22&lr=&num=100&as_brr=0&ei=aFVXS9O7H4T-ywTP-fiQCw&cd=1 book overview]</span> (revised edition; Plume; {{ISBN|0452284104}}, 9780452284104)
#*: ''Happy Days'' infamously '''jumped the shark''' when Fonzie literally jumped a shark on water skis. ''I Love Lucy'' '''jumped the shark''' when Lucy and Ricky moved to the suburbs. ''The Brady Bunch'' '''jumped the shark''' when Cousin Oliver moved in.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Ronn Owens|title=Voice of Reason: Why the Left and Right Are Wrong|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=4V59Uh7DpjQC&pg=PA212|page=212|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|year_published=2004|isbn=047148282X
|passage=From that point on, ''Dallas'' was never the same. It hit its peak. It lost credibility. It '''jumped the shark'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Michael Hewitt|title=The Watcher: Which new TV shows will take off?|titleurl=http://www.ocregister.com/entertainment/new-266787-season-family.html|journal=Orange County Register|date=16 September 2010
|passage=Returning show everyone will say has '''jumped the shark''': "House."}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|by extension}} To experience a [[decline]] in [[quality]], [[appeal]], [[popularity]], etc.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2007|author=w:Jeph Jacques|title=Time To Add A Cute Kid To The Cast|chapterurl=http://questionablecontent.net/view.php?comic=951|journal=Questionable Content|issue=951
|passage=Pintsize: Wait, don’t you want to know why I’m tied up and hanging from the ceiling?<br>Faye: Not really. Nighty night!<br>Pintsize: Shit! My wacky antics have '''jumped the shark'''!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Mark Fogarty|title=Went to See the Gypsy|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=T0euZbWPvXUC&pg=PA150&dq=%22jumped+the+shark%22|page=150|publisher=Lulu.com|year_published=2008|isbn=9781435751408
|passage=The Bangles were a fine all-woman band, starting with a lovely cover of Prince's "Manic Monday." "Walk Like an Egyptian" was cute, and the ladies rocked out an unlikely cover of Simon and Garfunkel's "Hazy Shade of Winter." But they '''jumped the shark''' with the treacly "Eternal Flame," and the end of the road was in sight.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Cortney Harding|title=Tape echo|titleurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=IBQEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA19&dq=%22jumped%20the%20shark%22|journal=Billboard|date=11 October 2008
|passage=And I knew something was up when I started getting press releases about "tape labels." Maybe vinyl had finally '''jumped the shark''', and magnetic tape was due for a return.}}

